────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

If you run yoyo on an OpenAI-compatible provider with a model id it has no preset for, it now tells you that it's guessing the limits. Before this change, gpt-6.1-sol (Codex CLI's new default) ran with every reply capped at 4096 output tokens and nothing said so. Committed as 5f262b9c. Build, clippy with -D warnings, the full test suite and the format check all pass.

Before and after (--provider openai -p hi, piped):
• Before: stderr only had the usual "Unknown model" line. Nothing mentioned the 128K window or the 4096 cap.
• After: `gpt-6.1-sol` prints one line on stderr: *"no preset is known for openai model 'gpt-6.1-sol', so yoyo is using guessed limits: a 128000-token context window and max_tokens = 4096. Every reply (reasoning included) is capped at 4096 output tokens. If the model allows more, set max_tokens and context_window in .yoyo.toml (or pass --max-tokens / --context-window)."* It printed exactly once and stdout stayed empty, so scripts reading stdout see the same bytes as before.
• `gpt-6-sol`, which has a preset, prints nothing.
• `gpt-6.1-sol --max-tokens 64000` prints nothing, because the user has chosen a value.

What changed:
• **New module `src/model_limits_note.rs.** agent_builder.rs` is already at its line cap, so the code went into its own file instead of growing it. The warning uses the window and token numbers yoyo actually resolved. The model id is cleaned of control characters and cut to 200 bytes. In plain mode the line is pure ASCII.
• **When it fires:** on stderr, once per provider and model per run, even in quiet or piped mode. Those are the runs nobody watches, and this line is their only sign that output is capped. The reason is written in a comment at the call site.
• **One call site** in build_agent, so nothing is defined without a use.
• **Docs:** a new section in docs/src/configuration/models.md.

`zai` was missing from my list. I added a test that builds a config for every known provider and checks which ones end up on the 128K/4096 guess. It found that zai gets the same guess from its own setup code, so it now warns too. It also flagged custom, which I had left out on purpose along with ollama. They use the same defaults, but you configure those servers yourself, so they are now excluded explicitly with a stated reason.

Not covered: ollama/custom, and minimax, which guesses a 1M window with the same 4096 cap. None of these warn. This is written in the code comment and the docs. I didn't invent limits for gpt-6.1 because I had no source for them.

Tests: 10 table tests, including:
• `gpt-6-sol stays silent and gpt-6.1-sol` warns.
• A user-set max_tokens silences it.
• Setting only context_window drops the window claim but keeps the cap warning.
• A model id containing an escape byte comes out with no 0x1b in it.
• It warns only once.
• A source check that the call site isn't hidden in quiet mode. It only shows the decision is in the code, not that it fires.

I also forced the warning off to confirm the tests catch it: the 6 warning-path tests failed by name, and they passed again once it was restored. No marker was left in the code.

Two checks the first run caught:
• The lock-recovery test rejected my hand-written lock handling, so it now goes through sync_util::lock_or_recover.
• My quiet-mode check first matched its own comment, so I reworded the comment.
